<h1 class="h-md">About StudentMind</h1>
<p class="lead">StudentMind helps university students understand how they think, learn and handle pressure, using short psychology tests and plain-language articles.</p>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card"><h3>1. Take a test</h3><p>Short statements, no jargon. You can try a test without an account.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><h3>2. Read your result</h3><p>See your scores and what they mean. Members can save results and track them over time.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><h3>3. Use the tips</h3><p>Each result comes with study advice, and the Learn section explains the ideas behind it.</p></div>
</div>
<h2 class="spaced">How the tests work</h2>
<p>The personality test uses 20 statements based on the Mini-IPIP, a short Big Five measure by Donnellan and colleagues (2006), built from the public-domain IPIP item pool. The exam stress, procrastination and career interest tests are informal self-checks written for this site. They are not clinical or validated scales.</p>
<h2>Your privacy</h2>
<p>Passwords are stored hashed. Only you can open your saved results on this site.</p>
<p class="muted">StudentMind is for education and self-awareness. It does not diagnose or treat any condition. If you are struggling, please talk to a university counselor or a doctor.</p>
